    "decoder_content_2": {
      "type": "rich-text",
      "blocks": {
        "heading": {
          "type": "heading",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "How to Read a Packaging Requirement Code"
          }
        },
        "text": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p>A typical MIL-STD-2073 code breaks down into a short string of letters, and each letter maps to an entry in Appendix J. Reading the letters in order usually reveals:</p><ul><li>The preservation method, which tells you how the item needs protection from moisture, corrosion, or physical damage</li><li>The cleaning and drying requirement that applies before preservation begins</li><li>The packing level, which sets how rugged the outer packaging needs to be for shipment and storage</li><li>Any special marking or unit pack instructions tied to that specific line item</li></ul><p>Once you identify those pieces, matching them to a barrier bag, roll stock material, or box becomes straightforward. The decoder above does this automatically, eliminating manual cross-referencing against a lengthy specification document.</p>"
          }
        }
      },
      "block_order": [
