      "blocks": {
        "faq_1": {
          "type": "faq",
          "settings": {
            "question": "What is MIL-STD-2073 used for?",
            "answer": "<p>MIL-STD-2073 sets the packaging, preservation, and marking requirements for military and defense items moving through the supply chain. The Department of Defense issues the standard, and contractors reference it whenever a contract or drawing calls for a specific packaging code.</p>"
          }
        },
        "faq_2": {
          "type": "faq",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Is MIL-STD-2073 the same as MIL-STD-129?",
            "answer": "<p>No, the two standards cover separate parts of the process. MIL-STD-2073 defines packaging, preservation, and unit pack requirements, and MIL-STD-129 covers military marking for shipment and storage.</p>"
          }
        },
        "faq_3": {
          "type": "faq",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Does every DoD contract require MIL-STD-2073 packaging?",
            "answer": "<p>Not every contract calls for it, so the packaging requirement code on your contract or drawing determines what applies. Check the specific line item before ordering materials to prevent delays later in production.</p>"
          }
        },
        "faq_4": {
          "type": "faq",
          "settings": {
            "question": "What happens if a shipment does not match its packaging code?",
            "answer": "<p>A mismatched shipment often gets rejected during inspection or returned for repackaging, which adds cost and delays delivery. Confirming the code before packing catches these issues early and keeps a contract on schedule.</p>"
          }
        },
        "faq_5": {
          "type": "faq",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Is the MIL-STD-2073 decoder free to use?",
            "answer": "<p>Yes, the decoder is free and available to anyone working with a MIL-STD-2073 packaging requirement code. No account or purchase is required to look up a translation.</p>"
          }
        }
      },
